From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: haroldxie <xiewenhao2308@icloud.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 19:04:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: kjit: keep fragment fixups until unregister has
 drained its calls

kjit_unregister_hook() turned off the static key and cleared the ops
pointer before it waited for the after_syscall calls in flight, and
search_kjit_extables() returned nothing once the key was off. A call in
flight may be running translated code, whose user accesses fault on first
touch of a page (demand paging, CoW) or under pagefault_disable(). Such a
fault during unregistration found no exception table entry, and the
kernel died with "access to user memory outside uaccess routines". Long
chained runs in the runtime made the window easy to hit with an rmmod
under load.

The invariant is that the fixups of translated code stay reachable until
no call that could be running it is left. Unregistering is now:

  static_branch_disable()   no new after_syscall calls
  synchronize_srcu()        calls in flight finish, ops still published
  rcu_assign_pointer(NULL)
  synchronize_srcu()        extable searches and task_work callbacks that
                            loaded the old ops finish

For the first grace period to cover every call, kjit_call_after_syscall()
now checks the static key inside its SRCU section: kjit_syscall_loop()
checks it only once, before its first call, and would otherwise keep
calling the hook, with the ops still published, after that grace period
had ended. A call that saw the key enabled started its SRCU section before
static_branch_disable() returned (the arm64 patching IPIs every CPU), so
the grace period waits for it.

search_kjit_extables() no longer looks at the static key, only at the
SRCU-protected ops pointer; the in_nmi() guard stays. With no runtime
registered, an address no other table claims now costs an SRCU read
section instead of a static branch; such lookups are rare (a kernel fault
about to oops, or a user fault that other tables do not cover).

Deferred task_work keeps its contract: it calls ops->task_work only while
the ops that queued it are published, and the second grace period waits
for any callback that loaded them, so no runtime code runs for a request
after kjit_unregister_hook() returns.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/kernel/kjit.c | 42 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kernel/kjit.c b/arch/arm64/kernel/kjit.c
index 5d8b034f1..e6413aeff 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/kjit.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/kjit.c
@@ -9,6 +9,14 @@
  * that implements them goes away. Syscalls the hook asks for are invoked
  * outside that section, so unregistering never waits for a blocked syscall.
  *
+ * A call to after_syscall may run translated code, and that code's user
+ * accesses fault (demand paging, CoW) and need search_kjit_extables() to find
+ * their fixups. So the ops pointer, which is all the extable search consults,
+ * stays published until every call that could be running translated code has
+ * returned: kjit_unregister_hook() first stops new calls (the static key,
+ * which kjit_call_after_syscall() checks inside its SRCU section), waits for
+ * the calls in flight, and only then clears the pointer.
+ *
  * Work the runtime defers to a task's own context (kjit_queue_task_work())
  * runs through kjit_task_work_fn(), which is kernel code: it calls into the
  * runtime under the same SRCU, and only if the registration that queued the
@@ -56,8 +64,23 @@ void kjit_unregister_hook(const struct kjit_hook_ops *ops)
 	guard(mutex)(&kjit_hook_mutex);
 	if (WARN_ON(rcu_access_pointer(kjit_hook) != ops))
 		return;
+	/*
+	 * No new after_syscall calls: kjit_call_after_syscall() checks the key
+	 * inside its SRCU section, so a call that still saw it enabled started
+	 * that section before this returned.
+	 */
 	static_branch_disable(&kjit_hook_enabled);
+	/*
+	 * The after_syscall calls in flight, and the translated code they run,
+	 * finish while @ops is still published, so their faults find their
+	 * fixups in search_kjit_extables().
+	 */
+	synchronize_srcu(&kjit_hook_srcu);
 	rcu_assign_pointer(kjit_hook, NULL);
+	/*
+	 * Extable searches and task_work callbacks that loaded @ops before it
+	 * was cleared finish.
+	 */
 	synchronize_srcu(&kjit_hook_srcu);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(kjit_unregister_hook);
@@ -69,9 +92,16 @@ long kjit_call_after_syscall(struct pt_regs *regs)
 	int idx;
 
 	idx = srcu_read_lock(&kjit_hook_srcu);
-	ops = srcu_dereference(kjit_hook, &kjit_hook_srcu);
-	if (ops)
-		scno = ops->after_syscall(regs);
+	/*
+	 * kjit_syscall_loop() checked the key once, before its first call; it
+	 * is checked again here, inside the SRCU section, so that no call
+	 * starts after kjit_unregister_hook()'s first grace period.
+	 */
+	if (static_branch_likely(&kjit_hook_enabled)) {
+		ops = srcu_dereference(kjit_hook, &kjit_hook_srcu);
+		if (ops)
+			scno = ops->after_syscall(regs);
+	}
 	srcu_read_unlock(&kjit_hook_srcu, idx);
 	return scno;
 }
@@ -123,6 +153,10 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(kjit_queue_task_work);
  * addresses no other table claims get here. SRCU readers are not NMI-safe;
  * translated code never runs in NMI context, so an NMI fault cannot be one of
  * its user accesses.
+ *
+ * Only the ops pointer decides, not the static key: translated code still
+ * running after the key was turned off must find its fixups, and
+ * kjit_unregister_hook() clears the pointer only once no such code runs.
  */
 const struct exception_table_entry *search_kjit_extables(unsigned long addr)
 {
@@ -130,7 +164,7 @@ const struct exception_table_entry *search_kjit_extables(unsigned long addr)
 	const struct kjit_hook_ops *ops;
 	int idx;
 
-	if (!static_branch_unlikely(&kjit_hook_enabled) || in_nmi())
+	if (in_nmi())
 		return NULL;
 
 	idx = srcu_read_lock(&kjit_hook_srcu);
